
(1) At build time, create a small shared component library (E.g. testing) that we create a python binding for.
This way, we don't have to reproduce / seperately maintain code which can create hidden regressions
(E.g. in generate_hashes.) Preferably, we would feed this back into the main build. If we are verifying behaviour
independently, then we may want to keep a seperate verification implementation (E.g. FNV-1A decoder) under a testing
subfolder, the verification algorithm we could itself check against the main c testing harness. The idea is we then
keep the python toolchain as thin as possible and in lockstep with the main codebase.

(2) Lut generator should defer to LLAC which will require us to begin contributing upstream again. For now, we have a simple
naive sin LUT.

(3) Do step-by-step code review starting from subtrees --> performance --> core --> generators --> testing.
We want to harden this toolchain quickly, as I don't want to really worry about devops stuff and just 'have it work.' That said,
parts of the toolchain, E.g. generators especially, are much more a part of the active codebase being more dynamic by design.

(4) We have standard CLI options like --dry-run, -P and --skip Etc. that we should uncouple into one class.

(5) We also want a standard design pattern for outputing raw json versus pretty printed output as per above ^^^.

(6) Tool to reduce repo size but only pulling what we use from third_party/ folders.

(7) Tool to reduce repo size but making the default resources/ files opt-in only (extra download.)
